---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Biology's Big Questions">
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">APS Framework</p>

      <h1>Biology's Big Questions</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        A guided entry point into the major questions of biology through the
        Agency–Process–Scale Framework.
      </p>

      <p class="hero-guidance">
        What is life? What makes something alive? Why do organisms appear
        goal-directed? What are genes, species, evolution, and biological meaning?
      </p>

      <p class="hero-guidance">
        APS approaches these questions through agency, process, and scale.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <div class="article-text-block">
        <h2>A Guided Entry Point</h2>

        <p>
          This page gathers the major public-facing questions addressed by the APS
          Framework. It is designed as a guided pathway rather than an alphabetical
          article index.
        </p>

        <p>
          Although these questions appear very different, APS treats them as
          connected. Questions about life, agency, goals, organisation, evolution,
          and meaning can be understood as different ways of examining how living
          systems persist, regulate themselves, and remain viable through time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <h2>Start Here: The APS Pathway</h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        For a first overview of APS, begin with life, then move through agency,
        goals, design, and biological meaning.
      </p>

      <ul class="card-list">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-life-a-biological-question-revisited/">
              What Is Life?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS approaches life as viability-oriented, constraint-closed
            organisation sustained through time.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/biological-agency-as-the-activity-of-self-maintenance/">
              What Is Biological Agency?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Biological agency is the activity through which living systems maintain,
            regulate, and organise themselves in relation to changing conditions.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/why-do-organisms-have-goals/">
              Why Do Organisms Have Goals?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS interprets biological goals through viability-oriented agency rather
            than conscious intention.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/design-in-nature-an-aps-clarification/">
              Is There Design in Nature?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Living systems often appear designed, but APS explains biological design
            through organised persistence rather than prior intention or planning.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-the-meaning-of-life/">
              What Is the Meaning of Life?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS connects biological meaning to the significance of living processes
            for viability, agency, and human interpretation.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <h2>Life</h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        These questions ask what life is, how living systems are recognised, and
        where the boundaries of life should be drawn.
      </p>

      <ul class="card-list">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-life-a-biological-question-revisited/">
              What Is Life?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Most definitions of life rely on lists of properties. APS approaches
            life as viability-oriented, constraint-closed organisation sustained
            through time.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/how-do-we-know-something-is-alive/">
              How Do We Know Something Is Alive?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Living systems must be distinguished from systems that merely appear
            alive. APS emphasises organisation, regulation, and persistence.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/are-viruses-alive/">
              Are Viruses Alive?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Viruses occupy one of biology's most controversial borderlands. APS
            examines whether they participate in organised persistence or depend
            upon it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-the-fundamental-unit-of-life/">
              What Is the Fundamental Unit of Life?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS treats organisms as major integrative hubs of biological agency,
            while avoiding the idea that life has only one privileged level.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <h2>Agency and Purpose</h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        These questions explain why living systems appear purposeful without
        appealing to external design or conscious intention.
      </p>

      <ul class="card-list">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/biological-agency-as-the-activity-of-self-maintenance/">
              What Is Biological Agency?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Biological agency names the self-maintaining, self-regulating activity
            through which living systems remain viable.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/why-do-organisms-have-goals/">
              Why Do Organisms Have Goals?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS understands biological goals as viability-oriented directions of
            activity rather than conscious plans.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/design-in-nature-an-aps-clarification/">
              Is There Design in Nature?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Biological design is treated as real but emergent: a product of
            organised persistence rather than an imposed blueprint.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-the-meaning-of-life/">
              What Is the Meaning of Life?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            APS distinguishes biological meaning from human meaning while showing
            how both are rooted in significance, relation, and agency.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <h2>Evolution and Organisation</h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        These questions examine how living systems change, diversify, and maintain
        coherence across generations.
      </p>

      <ul class="card-list">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/is-evolution-random/">
              Is Evolution Random?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Evolution involves variation, selection, inheritance, and organisation.
            It is neither wholly random nor wholly directed.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-a-gene/">
              What Is a Gene?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Genes are important biological resources, but they are not blueprints,
            instructions, or independent causal agents.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/what-is-a-species-in-aps/">
              What Is a Species?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Species are evolving lineages rather than fixed categories or immutable
            kinds.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <h2>Foundations of Biology</h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        These questions examine why biology requires its own explanatory concepts,
        even though living systems remain physical systems.
      </p>

      <ul class="card-list">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/why-is-biology-different-from-physics/">
              Why Is Biology Different From Physics?
            </a>
          </h3>
          <p class="card-excerpt">
            Biological systems obey physical laws while exhibiting forms of
            organisation, agency, and historical continuity that require
            specifically biological explanation.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <div class="article-text-block">
        <h2>How the Big Questions Connect</h2>

        <p>
          The major questions of biology are often treated as separate problems.
          APS connects them through a single explanatory pathway: life is understood
          through biological agency; agency gives rise to goal-directed activity;
          goal-directed activity helps explain organisation, evolution, cognition,
          and meaning.
        </p>

        <p>
          In this way, the Big Questions are not isolated topics. They are different
          entry points into the same larger problem: how living systems maintain
          themselves, transform through time, and generate forms of organisation
          that matter.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>